# scripts/research/nonmutable/microos.capabilities — R2 PROTOTYPE, not a fleet declaration
# (NON-MUTABLE-HOST-PROPOSAL.md §5 R2, #1004). What dotfiles-openSUSE's
# os/opensuse.capabilities WOULD become on MicroOS / Aeon (ID="opensuse-microos" /
# "opensuse-aeon", ID_LIKE carrying opensuse-tumbleweed — a Tumbleweed base, so `dup`),
# every value measured on a booted MicroOS 20260911 guest (research-nonmutable-vm.yml,
# runs 34819015395 and 34820719480) or marked "to verify".
#
# The R2 test against os/opensuse.capabilities:
#   PKG_INSTALL   `zypper in` → "Transactional system detected: A transactional-wrapper
#                 command is not installed. Please use transactional-update" (rc 5, every
#                 package, measured) — a refusal. The honest verb transacts into a NEW
#                 SNAPSHOT (measured: 21 s, exit 0, "Please reboot"); nothing is on PATH
#                 until PKG_APPLY.
#   PKG_UPGRADE   same shape: `transactional-update dup` stages, the reboot applies.
#   PKG_COUNT_PENDING
#                 transactional-update has NO check or dry-run mode (documented), so the
#                 count stays zypper's read-only list — TO VERIFY on the guest (the
#                 iteration-3 probe died of the harness's own SIGPIPE).
# Verdict for this family: additive — the honest install and upgrade verbs fit the
# existing keys once PKG_APPLY exists to say what happens next.

PROVISIONER=transactional

# ── package-manager verbs (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured on the guest: "All repositories have been refreshed" — the cache lives under
# /var, which is writable on the read-only root.
PKG_REFRESH=sudo zypper refresh
# INTERACTIVE, as the key requires: zypper asks inside the snapshot. `-n` cannot be
# appended by `up -y` (transactional-update takes its options BEFORE the command), so
# PKG_ASSUME_YES is omitted and `up -y` behaves like `up`. `transactional-update apply`
# exists (mounts the new snapshot's /usr /etc /boot live) but is documented as "not one
# atomic operation" — the reboot is the declaration.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo transactional-update dup
# Non-interactive, as the key requires; -n sits before the command. Measured: snapshot 2
# from 1, zypper inside it, "New default snapshot is #2 … Please reboot".
PKG_INSTALL=sudo transactional-update -n pkg in
PKG_REMOVE=sudo transactional-update -n pkg rm
PKG_SEARCH=zypper se
PKG_OWNS=zypper se --provides
# Measured on the guest (run 34852611338): `zypper --non-interactive lu` exits 0 —
# read-only against the running snapshot's repo cache, on the read-only root.
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=zypper -q list-updates
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^v[[:space:]]
PKG_PENDING_FS=|
PKG_PENDING_FIELD=3

# ── the staged change becomes live (prototype) ────────────────────────────────
PKG_APPLY=sudo systemctl reboot
# R5 (measured): transactional-update touches /run/reboot-needed when it closes a
# snapshot; a user can test it (0.0 s, exit 0 = staged). rebootmgrctl says the same
# ("Status: Reboot not requested" / requested) at the same cost. `snapper list` and
# `btrfs subvolume get-default` are root-only.
PKG_APPLY_PENDING=test -e /run/reboot-needed

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# No PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL: a snapshot is all-or-nothing, which is what "transactional"
# means. No MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE: MicroOS ships its OWN unattended
# transactional-update.timer + health-checker rollback; Core's runner must not run a
# second one beside it. Omitting the key is the refusal.

# ── optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Carried over from os/opensuse.capabilities unchanged: what is optional on the archive
# does not change with how the archive is applied.
TOOLS_OPTIN=lnav hyperfine watchexec shellcheck shfmt ouch git-absorb jnv gping uv

# ── `/etc` on this host (measured, for the driver's arm) ──────────────────────
# `/etc` is a per-snapshot overlay and IS writable in the running system: `chsh` took,
# `/etc/shells` accepted an entry. The documented caveat is now MEASURED (run 35130669056)
# and it is worse than a footnote for this fleet: a file changed both inside a staged
# snapshot and afterwards in the running system keeps ONLY the snapshot's copy, silently,
# on the next boot. A running-only edit made after the snapshot opened does carry forward,
# so the asymmetry is exactly as documented.
#
# THE DRIVER IS EXPOSED TO IT. `PKG_INSTALL` here is a STAGING verb, so every provisioning
# run leaves a snapshot open behind it — and `blib_set_login_shell` then appends to
# `/etc/shells` and runs `chsh` into a copy of `/etc` the next boot discards. Both writes
# were measured lost that way. The remedy belongs in dotfiles-openSUSE's transactional arm
# (order the writes before the staging verb, or re-apply after PKG_APPLY), not in a
# declaration key — tracked as dotfiles-openSUSE#199.
#
# Two `transactional-update` facts from the same run: `dup` REFUSES outright when any
# enabled repo fails to refresh (zypper exit 4) and DELETES its own snapshot on the way
# out — so the transition those markers crossed was the preceding `run` snapshot, not a
# completed `dup`. The overlay mechanism does not depend on which verb opened the snapshot.
